---
title: Custom Extensions
description: Extend TanStack Charts with custom marks, distinct scale values, curves, scales, color, legends, text measurement, spatial indexes, and renderers.
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Charts exposes narrow inversion-of-control boundaries around its\nscene compiler. Prefer composition with built-in marks first. Add an extension\nwhen the chart requires geometry or behavior that cannot be expressed without\ndistorting its data model."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"composite-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Composite marks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compositeMark"},{"type":"text","value":" groups ordinary marks behind one stable parent identity:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { compositeMark } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/composite'\n\nfunction compositeMark<\n  TMarks extends readonly ChartMark<any, any, any, any, any>[],\n>(\n  marks: TMarks,\n  options?: CompositeMarkOptions<ChartMarkDatum<TMarks[number]>>,\n): ChartMark<\n  ChartMarkDatum<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkPointX<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkPointY<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkScaleX<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkScaleY<TMarks[number]>\n>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CompositeMarkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":" contains optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"id"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" fields.\nThe result preserves the union of child datum and positional types. Initialization merges\nthe children's semantic channels under parent and child namespaces. Rendering\nkeeps child order, namespaces scene keys and mark IDs, and retains each child\npoint as a separate interaction target. Parent and child motion definitions\nmerge under the resolved child namespace, with child fields taking precedence."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every child must have a unique ID and an ordinary initialized "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" method.\nA child that owns "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" is rejected; keep one resolved-layout owner\ninstead of nesting scheduling lifecycles. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md#group-reusable-child-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Marks and Renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor composition guidance."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-marks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom marks"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMark } from '@tanstack/charts'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function createMark<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TXScaleId extends string = 'x',\n  TYScaleId extends string = 'y',\n>(\n  initialize: (\n    context: MarkInitializeContext,\n  ) => MarkInitialization<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>,\n  renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue, TXValue, TYValue, TXScaleId, TYScaleId>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Initialization runs once per scene compilation and receives the mark's layer\nindex:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MarkInitializeContext {\n  markIndex: number\n}\n\ninterface InitializedMark<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  id: string\n  channels: Readonly<Record<string, MaterializedChannel>>\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<any>\n  viewport?: Readonly<Partial<Record<'x' | 'y', 'content' | 'fixed'>>>\n  focusGuideOnly?: boolean\n  layoutLabels?(context: MarkRenderContext): readonly SceneLabel[]\n  render(context: MarkRenderContext): MarkScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  resolveLayout?(\n    context: MarkResolvedLayoutContext,\n  ): ResolvedMarkLayout<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkInitialization"},{"type":"text","value":" also accepts a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedLayoutMarkInitialization"},{"type":"text","value":", which\nhas "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" instead of an initial "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMark"},{"type":"text","value":" normalizes both\nforms to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"InitializedMark"},{"type":"text","value":", so wrappers around ordinary marks retain a callable\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional factory "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" is copied onto each initialized mark. An\ninitializer may return its own "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"motion"},{"type":"text","value":" when a composite or resolved layout\nneeds a scene-local policy; that value takes precedence over the factory\nfallback."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional third "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" argument is copied onto the nodes returned by\nboth direct "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" and resolved-layout render paths. It does not replace or\nshift the motion argument. Use a stable "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" instance so an\nupdate can reuse its existing surface composition."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Materialized channels declare semantic values before scale resolution:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MaterializedChannel {\n  scale?: string\n  values: readonly unknown[]\n  includeZero?: boolean\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpec.scales"},{"type":"text","value":" ID for a positional channel. The reserved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":"\nIDs are the defaults; additional IDs select named scales. Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale: 'color'"},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the shared color scale, and do not reuse "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"color"},{"type":"text","value":" as a positional scale ID.\nPass named position IDs through the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMark"},{"type":"text","value":" scale ID type parameters so\ntheir values do not widen the reserved scale types. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"includeZero"},{"type":"text","value":" is a hint\navailable to a custom scale resolver. Filter invalid values before\nmaterializing them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" overrides presentation ownership independently for x and y. Without\nan override, a mark is viewport content on an axis when one of its materialized\nchannels uses that axis. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'content'"},{"type":"text","value":" forces the mark into that axis's clipped,\ntranslated layer even when no channel establishes the relationship. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'fixed'"},{"type":"text","value":"\nkeeps the mark stationary on that axis even when a channel contributes to its\ndomain. This ownership does not change scale-domain contribution."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The exported "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartContinuousDomain<TValue>"},{"type":"text","value":" type represents viewport state as\neither "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readonly [number, number]"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"readonly [Date, Date]"},{"type":"text","value":", narrowed by the\nmark and axis value type. Numeric and temporal endpoints cannot be mixed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Render nodes and points with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.scales[axis].map"},{"type":"text","value":". The scene compiler\napplies the mark's viewport translation and remaps its interaction-point\nreferences. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"scale.viewport?.map"},{"type":"text","value":" is for consumers that need the final\npresented coordinate outside mark rendering."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The render context provides final geometry and shared presentation:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MarkRenderContext {\n  markIndex: number\n  surface: ChartBounds\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  color(value: ChartKey | null | undefined): string\n  colors: ResolvedColorScale\n  layout: ChartLayoutOptions\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"final-screen-mark-layout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Final-screen mark layout"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" when binning, collision avoidance, topology, or responsive\npacking depends on final positional scales and inner bounds:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MarkResolvedLayoutContext {\n  markIndex: number\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  layout: ChartLayoutOptions\n}\n\ninterface ResolvedMarkLayout<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue> {\n  channels?: Readonly<Record<string, MaterializedChannel>>\n  states?: InitializedMark<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>['states']\n  layoutLabels?(context: MarkRenderContext): readonly SceneLabel[]\n  render(context: MarkRenderContext): MarkScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The margin solver may call "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" more than once. Keep it synchronous,\npure, deterministic, and free of application state. Initial channels alone\nestablish x/y domains. Resolved channels replace them for final\nnon-positional inference, including color; resolved x/y values never re-domain\nthe positional scales. Derived rows stay inside the returned render closure\ninstead of becoming a cross-mark transform graph."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If a custom mark emits labels that should participate in automatic margins,\nreturn the same positioned labels from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"layoutLabels"},{"type":"text","value":". The solver may call it\nmore than once with different responsive ranges; keep it pure. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":" still\nruns once with the final layout. The built-in Cartesian "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"text"},{"type":"text","value":" mark provides\nthis hook."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Return keyed scene nodes and, when the mark participates in native\ninteraction, typed points:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface MarkScene<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  nodes: readonly SceneNode[]\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  focusAnchors?: readonly ChartFocusAnchor[]\n  focusGuides?: readonly MarkFocusGuide[]\n}\n\ntype MarkFocusGuide = Omit<SceneFocusGuide, 'placement'> & {\n  placement?: SceneFocusGuide['placement']\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a datum composed of several leaves, put state metadata on its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneGroup"},{"type":"text","value":"\nand set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"states.target: 'group'"},{"type":"text","value":". The resolver applies paint to that group once\nand leaves its children unchanged. This gives a candle's body and wicks one\nopacity transition instead of three. A child group with its own states still\nresolves independently. Omit "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"target"},{"type":"text","value":", or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'children'"},{"type":"text","value":", for the existing\nstate inheritance behavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Group state metadata contains the original "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"data"},{"type":"text","value":", ordered "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"definitions"},{"type":"text","value":", and\nthe "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"points"},{"type":"text","value":" that identify its datum. Those state candidates alone do not add\npointer or keyboard targets. Group states support paint; geometry properties\nsuch as dot radius and text rotation apply only to their corresponding leaves."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointOwner"},{"type":"text","value":" identifies one source point for decorative geometry. Use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointOwners"},{"type":"text","value":" for a fill or path owned by several points. These fields preserve\nsemantic ownership for state and focus resolution, they do not add hit-test\npoints. Scene adoption and composite namespacing transform them with the\ncorresponding source points."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneLabel"},{"type":"text","value":" may supply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusOpacity(context)"},{"type":"text","value":" for presentation-only\nopacity. The context contains current "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focus"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pointer"},{"type":"text","value":". Built-in tick\nlabels use this callback during focus painting, leaving their layout and\ncollision thinning unchanged. Their base style stores the initial opacity,\nwhich is restored when focus clears. Custom renderers must honor this scene\ncallback when implementing inline state painting."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Browser control extensions receive "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setStateFocus(focus)"},{"type":"text","value":" from their host.\nIt overrides only inline-state matching, not interaction focus, tooltip\ncontent, cursor state, or focus-filtered geometry. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":" to restore\nmatching against real interaction focus. Controls own refreshing that override\nwhen their scene changes and clearing it on removal. The context and lifecycle\ntypes are exported as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartHostControlExtensionContext"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartHostControlExtension"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartHostControlInstance"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"decorative(mark)"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/mark/decorative"},{"type":"text","value":" retains inline states\nand source ownership while removing pointer and keyboard targets. For a\ncomposite mark, wrap the complete composite rather than its children. A\ndecorative mark still cannot contain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused()"},{"type":"text","value":" geometry, directly or\ninside a composite. These combinations are rejected by TypeScript when the\nmark's inferred type is retained. JavaScript consumers and custom marks with\nwidened types are checked at runtime during initialization or scene rendering."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusAnchors"},{"type":"text","value":" let "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"whenFocused"},{"type":"text","value":" reveal decorative geometry without making it\na pointer or keyboard target. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuides"},{"type":"text","value":" describe data-less presentation\nresolved from current focus or cursor state. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" has the final\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" fields except that "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":" is optional. Omit it to let\nmark order place a guide under or over the first ordinary mark. Supply it only\nwhen a composed nested scene must preserve an already resolved placement.\nEvery focus guide supplies a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolve"},{"type":"text","value":" callback that receives\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneFocusGuideResolveContext"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns one transient "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneNode"},{"type":"text","value":" or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"undefined"},{"type":"text","value":". The callback stays attached to the optional guide instead of\nbecoming unconditional renderer policy. Import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveCrosshairGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/crosshair"},{"type":"text","value":" to reuse the built-in rule, band, label, and marker\nbehavior."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Set "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"focusGuideOnly: true"},{"type":"text","value":" on an initialized mark that contributes only these\ndynamic guides and no ordinary base-scene content. This explicit\nclassification keeps the mark out of the first-ordinary-mark boundary used by\ndefault placement. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"surface"},{"type":"text","value":" is the required full-surface bounds; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"},{"type":"text","value":" is\nthe inner plot. Guide rules normally clip to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"chart"},{"type":"text","value":", while labels may use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"surface"},{"type":"text","value":" for clamping."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The practical contracts are in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Marks and Renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"mark-requirements","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark requirements"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give the mark a stable ID. Derive a fallback from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"markIndex"},{"type":"text","value":" only when layer\norder is stable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Materialize every value needed to establish positional domains during\ninitialization."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Map through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.scales"},{"type":"text","value":"; do not recalculate responsive ranges."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Declare "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewport"},{"type":"text","value":" ownership when channel inference does not match the\nmark's content or fixed annotation behavior."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give each scene node and point a deterministic key."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep presentation-only focus anchors keyed to the nodes they reveal."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Emit finite geometry only."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve the original datum and index in every interaction point."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use one honest focus coordinate and semantic x/y pair per point."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep semantic row transforms eager and outside "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"render"},{"type":"text","value":"; use\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveLayout"},{"type":"text","value":" only for work that requires final screen geometry."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The scene node and point shapes are documented in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./runtime-and-scene.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Runtime and scene"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"distinct-point-and-scale-values","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Distinct point and scale values"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interval geometry may materialize endpoint value types that differ from its\ninteraction anchor types. Use the exceptional subpath:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createMarkWithScaleValues } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/scale-values'","lang":"ts"},{"type":"code","value":"function createMarkWithScaleValues<\n  TDatum,\n  TXPointValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYPointValue extends ChartValue,\n  TXScaleValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYScaleValue extends ChartValue,\n  TXScaleId extends string = 'x',\n  TYScaleId extends string = 'y',\n>(\n  initialize: (\n    context: MarkInitializeContext,\n  ) => MarkInitialization<TDatum, TXPointValue, TYPointValue>,\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>,\n  renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer,\n): ChartMark<\n  TDatum,\n  TXPointValue,\n  TYPointValue,\n  TXScaleValue,\n  TYScaleValue,\n  TXScaleId,\n  TYScaleId\n>","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The subpath also exports "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkPointX"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkPointY"},{"type":"text","value":",\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkScaleX"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkScaleY"},{"type":"text","value":". Use it only when the distinction is\nreal; ordinary custom marks should use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createMark"},{"type":"text","value":". Pass named scale IDs in\nthe last two type parameters when the mark does not use reserved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"x"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"y"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"curves","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Curves"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartCurve"},{"type":"text","value":" supplies precomputed path data for line and y-oriented area marks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface ChartCurve {\n  line(points: readonly (readonly [number, number])[]): string\n  area(\n    top: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n    bottom: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n  ): string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"AreaXCurve"},{"type":"text","value":" has the transposed contract:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"interface AreaXCurve {\n  areaX(\n    right: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n    left: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n  ): string\n}","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The optional bridges "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3Curve"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/d3/shape"},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"d3AreaXCurve"},{"type":"text","value":" from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/d3/area-x"},{"type":"text","value":" adapt a supplied curve factory\nto these contracts. D3 module ownership and granular imports are documented in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-positional-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom positional scales"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A custom "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartScale"},{"type":"text","value":" resolves semantic values and the responsive range into a\ncomplete mapping and tick set. This is an unchecked math boundary; prefer a\nconfigured callable scale when possible."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./scales-guides-and-color.md#custom-scales","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the exact context and return type."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-color-scales-and-legends","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom color scales and legends"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorScale"},{"type":"text","value":" maps observed values, domain/range hints, and theme tokens to\na "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ResolvedColorScale"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartColorLegend"},{"type":"text","value":" independently reserves layout height\nand emits a scene node."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./scales-guides-and-color.md#color","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Color"}]},{"type":"text","value":" and\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./scales-guides-and-color.md#custom-legends","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom legends"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-text-measurement","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom text measurement"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartTextMeasurer"},{"type":"text","value":" lets nonbrowser rendering, special fonts, or an\napplication-owned typography engine provide painted glyph bounds. It affects\nguide geometry, not mark text rendering. It is synchronous and receives the\ncomplete resolved "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartTextTypography"},{"type":"text","value":"; hosts re-render after asynchronous\nfont readiness changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./scales-guides-and-color.md#automatic-guide-layout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Automatic guide layout"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the contract."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"spatial-indexes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spatial indexes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSpatialIndexFactory"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the default linear pointer lookup without\nchanging scene compilation. Build a point-only index from its first argument,\nor use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"context.scene"},{"type":"text","value":" from its second argument to index resolved primitive\nbounds. Return the nearest original point within the requested distance. The\nhost recreates the index when the scene or factory changes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./focus-and-interaction.md#spatial-indexes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Spatial indexes"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The\nappropriate granular spatial primitive can be brought through the boundary\ndescribed in "},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/scales-and-d3.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scales"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-focus-and-gestures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom focus and gestures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartFocusStrategy"},{"type":"text","value":" owns pointer resolution, focus grouping, and keyboard task\norder. Its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolve(points, context)"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"group(points, context)"},{"type":"text","value":" methods keep\ncoordinates and the active point in named context bags. Rich gestures can\ninstead disable chart-owned focus and maintain selection or viewport state in\nthe application."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"./focus-and-interaction.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus and interaction"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-renderers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom renderers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" owns both deterministic server markup and one mounted\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSurface"},{"type":"text","value":". The surface renders scenes, converts browser coordinates to\nscene coordinates, paints focus, and releases renderer-owned resources.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"mountChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" keeps responsive sizing, runtime updates, focus,\nkeyboard, tooltip, and selection behavior shared across renderers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom surfaces should resolve authored focus layers and data-less guides with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusPresentation(scene, focus, pointer, cursor)"},{"type":"text","value":", then paint its\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"under"},{"type":"text","value":" nodes, the base scene, and its "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"over"},{"type":"text","value":" nodes in that order."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The scene compiler has already converted every mark-emitted "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MarkFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":"\nto a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"SceneFocusGuide"},{"type":"text","value":" with required "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"placement"},{"type":"text","value":" before a renderer receives the\nscene. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"resolveFocusPresentation"},{"type":"text","value":" calls each guide's resolver with its local\nfocus, pointer, and cursor context. A custom renderer should consume those\nresolved nodes and final placement through that helper; it should not call\nguide resolvers or infer mark order again."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/renderer"},{"type":"text","value":" directly or the framework "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"/core"},{"type":"text","value":" entries.\nThe optional built-in implementation at "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/canvas"},{"type":"text","value":" demonstrates\nthe boundary without changing the default SVG imports."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" is the small renderer-selection token stored in universal\nmark and scene types. A DOM implementation uses "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartLayerRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":", which\nextends "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"compose(defaultRenderer)"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"UniversalChartLayerRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" provides the same\ncontract for a definition-agnostic renderer. The returned compositor owns the\nordered child surfaces and exposes them through "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSurface.layers"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Built-in marks accept a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" through their shared\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartMarkOptions"},{"type":"text","value":". Custom marks pass it as the third factory argument, after\nthe optional motion definition. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./rendering-and-export.md#mark-level-renderers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mark-level renderers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For an SVG-only serialization change, pass a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ChartSvgRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"renderSvg"},{"type":"text","value":"\nto the compatibility host or adapt it with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createSvgChartRenderer"},{"type":"text","value":" from\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/charts/svg/renderer"},{"type":"text","value":". Preserve the SVG root, stable DOM keys,\naccessible name, coordinate system, and focus presentation expected by that\nadapter."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Default SVG serialization already preserves declared gradients and group\nclips; see "},{"type":"link","href":"./rendering-and-export.md#svg-resources","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rendering and export"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"composite-marks","text":"Composite marks","level":2},{"id":"custom-marks","text":"Custom marks","level":2},{"id":"final-screen-mark-layout","text":"Final-screen mark layout","level":3},{"id":"mark-requirements","text":"Mark requirements","level":3},{"id":"distinct-point-and-scale-values","text":"Distinct point and scale values","level":2},{"id":"curves","text":"Curves","level":2},{"id":"custom-positional-scales","text":"Custom positional scales","level":2},{"id":"custom-color-scales-and-legends","text":"Custom color scales and legends","level":2},{"id":"custom-text-measurement","text":"Custom text measurement","level":2},{"id":"spatial-indexes","text":"Spatial indexes","level":2},{"id":"custom-focus-and-gestures","text":"Custom focus and gestures","level":2},{"id":"custom-renderers","text":"Custom renderers","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Charts exposes narrow inversion-of-control boundaries around its\nscene compiler. Prefer composition with built-in marks first. Add an extension\nwhen the chart requires geometry or behavior that cannot be expressed without\ndistorting its data model.\n\n## Composite marks\n\n`compositeMark` groups ordinary marks behind one stable parent identity:\n\n```ts\nimport { compositeMark } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/composite'\n\nfunction compositeMark<\n  TMarks extends readonly ChartMark<any, any, any, any, any>[],\n>(\n  marks: TMarks,\n  options?: CompositeMarkOptions<ChartMarkDatum<TMarks[number]>>,\n): ChartMark<\n  ChartMarkDatum<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkPointX<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkPointY<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkScaleX<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkScaleY<TMarks[number]>\n>\n```\n\n`CompositeMarkOptions` contains optional `id`, `motion`, and `renderer` fields.\nThe result preserves the union of child datum and positional types. Initialization merges\nthe children's semantic channels under parent and child namespaces. Rendering\nkeeps child order, namespaces scene keys and mark IDs, and retains each child\npoint as a separate interaction target. Parent and child motion definitions\nmerge under the resolved child namespace, with child fields taking precedence.\n\nEvery child must have a unique ID and an ordinary initialized `render` method.\nA child that owns `resolveLayout` is rejected; keep one resolved-layout owner\ninstead of nesting scheduling lifecycles. See\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md#group-reusable-child-marks)\nfor composition guidance.\n\n## Custom marks\n\n```ts\nimport { createMark } from '@tanstack/charts'\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createMark<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TXScaleId extends string = 'x',\n  TYScaleId extends string = 'y',\n>(\n  initialize: (\n    context: MarkInitializeContext,\n  ) => MarkInitialization<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>,\n  renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue, TXValue, TYValue, TXScaleId, TYScaleId>\n```\n\nInitialization runs once per scene compilation and receives the mark's layer\nindex:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkInitializeContext {\n  markIndex: number\n}\n\ninterface InitializedMark<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  id: string\n  channels: Readonly<Record<string, MaterializedChannel>>\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<any>\n  viewport?: Readonly<Partial<Record<'x' | 'y', 'content' | 'fixed'>>>\n  focusGuideOnly?: boolean\n  layoutLabels?(context: MarkRenderContext): readonly SceneLabel[]\n  render(context: MarkRenderContext): MarkScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  resolveLayout?(\n    context: MarkResolvedLayoutContext,\n  ): ResolvedMarkLayout<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n}\n```\n\n`MarkInitialization` also accepts a `ResolvedLayoutMarkInitialization`, which\nhas `resolveLayout` instead of an initial `render`. `createMark` normalizes both\nforms to `InitializedMark`, so wrappers around ordinary marks retain a callable\n`render`.\n\nThe optional factory `motion` is copied onto each initialized mark. An\ninitializer may return its own `motion` when a composite or resolved layout\nneeds a scene-local policy; that value takes precedence over the factory\nfallback.\n\nThe optional third `renderer` argument is copied onto the nodes returned by\nboth direct `render` and resolved-layout render paths. It does not replace or\nshift the motion argument. Use a stable `ChartMarkRenderer` instance so an\nupdate can reuse its existing surface composition.\n\nMaterialized channels declare semantic values before scale resolution:\n\n```ts\ninterface MaterializedChannel {\n  scale?: string\n  values: readonly unknown[]\n  includeZero?: boolean\n}\n```\n\nUse a `ChartSpec.scales` ID for a positional channel. The reserved `x` and `y`\nIDs are the defaults; additional IDs select named scales. Use `scale: 'color'`\nfor the shared color scale, and do not reuse `color` as a positional scale ID.\nPass named position IDs through the `createMark` scale ID type parameters so\ntheir values do not widen the reserved scale types. `includeZero` is a hint\navailable to a custom scale resolver. Filter invalid values before\nmaterializing them.\n\n`viewport` overrides presentation ownership independently for x and y. Without\nan override, a mark is viewport content on an axis when one of its materialized\nchannels uses that axis. `'content'` forces the mark into that axis's clipped,\ntranslated layer even when no channel establishes the relationship. `'fixed'`\nkeeps the mark stationary on that axis even when a channel contributes to its\ndomain. This ownership does not change scale-domain contribution.\n\nThe exported `ChartContinuousDomain<TValue>` type represents viewport state as\neither `readonly [number, number]` or `readonly [Date, Date]`, narrowed by the\nmark and axis value type. Numeric and temporal endpoints cannot be mixed.\n\nRender nodes and points with `context.scales[axis].map`. The scene compiler\napplies the mark's viewport translation and remaps its interaction-point\nreferences. `scale.viewport?.map` is for consumers that need the final\npresented coordinate outside mark rendering.\n\nThe render context provides final geometry and shared presentation:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkRenderContext {\n  markIndex: number\n  surface: ChartBounds\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  color(value: ChartKey | null | undefined): string\n  colors: ResolvedColorScale\n  layout: ChartLayoutOptions\n}\n```\n\n### Final-screen mark layout\n\nUse `resolveLayout` when binning, collision avoidance, topology, or responsive\npacking depends on final positional scales and inner bounds:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkResolvedLayoutContext {\n  markIndex: number\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  layout: ChartLayoutOptions\n}\n\ninterface ResolvedMarkLayout<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue> {\n  channels?: Readonly<Record<string, MaterializedChannel>>\n  states?: InitializedMark<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>['states']\n  layoutLabels?(context: MarkRenderContext): readonly SceneLabel[]\n  render(context: MarkRenderContext): MarkScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n}\n```\n\nThe margin solver may call `resolveLayout` more than once. Keep it synchronous,\npure, deterministic, and free of application state. Initial channels alone\nestablish x/y domains. Resolved channels replace them for final\nnon-positional inference, including color; resolved x/y values never re-domain\nthe positional scales. Derived rows stay inside the returned render closure\ninstead of becoming a cross-mark transform graph.\n\nIf a custom mark emits labels that should participate in automatic margins,\nreturn the same positioned labels from `layoutLabels`. The solver may call it\nmore than once with different responsive ranges; keep it pure. `render` still\nruns once with the final layout. The built-in Cartesian `text` mark provides\nthis hook.\n\nReturn keyed scene nodes and, when the mark participates in native\ninteraction, typed points:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkScene<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  nodes: readonly SceneNode[]\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  focusAnchors?: readonly ChartFocusAnchor[]\n  focusGuides?: readonly MarkFocusGuide[]\n}\n\ntype MarkFocusGuide = Omit<SceneFocusGuide, 'placement'> & {\n  placement?: SceneFocusGuide['placement']\n}\n```\n\nFor a datum composed of several leaves, put state metadata on its `SceneGroup`\nand set `states.target: 'group'`. The resolver applies paint to that group once\nand leaves its children unchanged. This gives a candle's body and wicks one\nopacity transition instead of three. A child group with its own states still\nresolves independently. Omit `target`, or use `'children'`, for the existing\nstate inheritance behavior.\n\nGroup state metadata contains the original `data`, ordered `definitions`, and\nthe `points` that identify its datum. Those state candidates alone do not add\npointer or keyboard targets. Group states support paint; geometry properties\nsuch as dot radius and text rotation apply only to their corresponding leaves.\n\n`pointOwner` identifies one source point for decorative geometry. Use\n`pointOwners` for a fill or path owned by several points. These fields preserve\nsemantic ownership for state and focus resolution, they do not add hit-test\npoints. Scene adoption and composite namespacing transform them with the\ncorresponding source points.\n\nA `SceneLabel` may supply `focusOpacity(context)` for presentation-only\nopacity. The context contains current `focus` and `pointer`. Built-in tick\nlabels use this callback during focus painting, leaving their layout and\ncollision thinning unchanged. Their base style stores the initial opacity,\nwhich is restored when focus clears. Custom renderers must honor this scene\ncallback when implementing inline state painting.\n\nBrowser control extensions receive `setStateFocus(focus)` from their host.\nIt overrides only inline-state matching, not interaction focus, tooltip\ncontent, cursor state, or focus-filtered geometry. Pass `null` to restore\nmatching against real interaction focus. Controls own refreshing that override\nwhen their scene changes and clearing it on removal. The context and lifecycle\ntypes are exported as `ChartHostControlExtensionContext`,\n`ChartHostControlExtension`, and `ChartHostControlInstance`.\n\n`decorative(mark)` from `@tanstack/charts/mark/decorative` retains inline states\nand source ownership while removing pointer and keyboard targets. For a\ncomposite mark, wrap the complete composite rather than its children. A\ndecorative mark still cannot contain `whenFocused()` geometry, directly or\ninside a composite. These combinations are rejected by TypeScript when the\nmark's inferred type is retained. JavaScript consumers and custom marks with\nwidened types are checked at runtime during initialization or scene rendering.\n\n`focusAnchors` let `whenFocused` reveal decorative geometry without making it\na pointer or keyboard target. `focusGuides` describe data-less presentation\nresolved from current focus or cursor state. `MarkFocusGuide` has the final\n`SceneFocusGuide` fields except that `placement` is optional. Omit it to let\nmark order place a guide under or over the first ordinary mark. Supply it only\nwhen a composed nested scene must preserve an already resolved placement.\nEvery focus guide supplies a `resolve` callback that receives\n`SceneFocusGuideResolveContext` and returns one transient `SceneNode` or\n`undefined`. The callback stays attached to the optional guide instead of\nbecoming unconditional renderer policy. Import `resolveCrosshairGuide` from\n`@tanstack/charts/crosshair` to reuse the built-in rule, band, label, and marker\nbehavior.\n\nSet `focusGuideOnly: true` on an initialized mark that contributes only these\ndynamic guides and no ordinary base-scene content. This explicit\nclassification keeps the mark out of the first-ordinary-mark boundary used by\ndefault placement. `surface` is the required full-surface bounds; `chart` is\nthe inner plot. Guide rules normally clip to `chart`, while labels may use\n`surface` for clamping.\n\nThe practical contracts are in\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md).\n\n### Mark requirements\n\n- Give the mark a stable ID. Derive a fallback from `markIndex` only when layer\n  order is stable.\n- Materialize every value needed to establish positional domains during\n  initialization.\n- Map through `context.scales`; do not recalculate responsive ranges.\n- Declare `viewport` ownership when channel inference does not match the\n  mark's content or fixed annotation behavior.\n- Give each scene node and point a deterministic key.\n- Keep presentation-only focus anchors keyed to the nodes they reveal.\n- Emit finite geometry only.\n- Preserve the original datum and index in every interaction point.\n- Use one honest focus coordinate and semantic x/y pair per point.\n- Keep semantic row transforms eager and outside `render`; use\n  `resolveLayout` only for work that requires final screen geometry.\n\nThe scene node and point shapes are documented in\n[Runtime and scene](./runtime-and-scene.md).\n\n## Distinct point and scale values\n\nInterval geometry may materialize endpoint value types that differ from its\ninteraction anchor types. Use the exceptional subpath:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMarkWithScaleValues } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/scale-values'\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createMarkWithScaleValues<\n  TDatum,\n  TXPointValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYPointValue extends ChartValue,\n  TXScaleValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYScaleValue extends ChartValue,\n  TXScaleId extends string = 'x',\n  TYScaleId extends string = 'y',\n>(\n  initialize: (\n    context: MarkInitializeContext,\n  ) => MarkInitialization<TDatum, TXPointValue, TYPointValue>,\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>,\n  renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer,\n): ChartMark<\n  TDatum,\n  TXPointValue,\n  TYPointValue,\n  TXScaleValue,\n  TYScaleValue,\n  TXScaleId,\n  TYScaleId\n>\n```\n\nThe subpath also exports `ChartMarkPointX`, `ChartMarkPointY`,\n`ChartMarkScaleX`, and `ChartMarkScaleY`. Use it only when the distinction is\nreal; ordinary custom marks should use `createMark`. Pass named scale IDs in\nthe last two type parameters when the mark does not use reserved `x` or `y`.\n\n## Curves\n\n`ChartCurve` supplies precomputed path data for line and y-oriented area marks:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartCurve {\n  line(points: readonly (readonly [number, number])[]): string\n  area(\n    top: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n    bottom: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n  ): string\n}\n```\n\n`AreaXCurve` has the transposed contract:\n\n```ts\ninterface AreaXCurve {\n  areaX(\n    right: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n    left: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n  ): string\n}\n```\n\nThe optional bridges `d3Curve` from `@tanstack/charts/d3/shape` and\n`d3AreaXCurve` from `@tanstack/charts/d3/area-x` adapt a supplied curve factory\nto these contracts. D3 module ownership and granular imports are documented in\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Custom positional scales\n\nA custom `ChartScale` resolves semantic values and the responsive range into a\ncomplete mapping and tick set. This is an unchecked math boundary; prefer a\nconfigured callable scale when possible.\n\nSee [Custom scales](./scales-guides-and-color.md#custom-scales)\nfor the exact context and return type.\n\n## Custom color scales and legends\n\n`ChartColorScale` maps observed values, domain/range hints, and theme tokens to\na `ResolvedColorScale`. `ChartColorLegend` independently reserves layout height\nand emits a scene node.\n\nSee [Color](./scales-guides-and-color.md#color) and\n[Custom legends](./scales-guides-and-color.md#custom-legends).\n\n## Custom text measurement\n\n`ChartTextMeasurer` lets nonbrowser rendering, special fonts, or an\napplication-owned typography engine provide painted glyph bounds. It affects\nguide geometry, not mark text rendering. It is synchronous and receives the\ncomplete resolved `ChartTextTypography`; hosts re-render after asynchronous\nfont readiness changes.\n\nSee [Automatic guide layout](./scales-guides-and-color.md#automatic-guide-layout)\nfor the contract.\n\n## Spatial indexes\n\n`ChartSpatialIndexFactory` replaces the default linear pointer lookup without\nchanging scene compilation. Build a point-only index from its first argument,\nor use `context.scene` from its second argument to index resolved primitive\nbounds. Return the nearest original point within the requested distance. The\nhost recreates the index when the scene or factory changes.\n\nSee [Spatial indexes](./focus-and-interaction.md#spatial-indexes). The\nappropriate granular spatial primitive can be brought through the boundary\ndescribed in [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Custom focus and gestures\n\n`ChartFocusStrategy` owns pointer resolution, focus grouping, and keyboard task\norder. Its `resolve(points, context)` and `group(points, context)` methods keep\ncoordinates and the active point in named context bags. Rich gestures can\ninstead disable chart-owned focus and maintain selection or viewport state in\nthe application.\n\nSee [Focus and interaction](./focus-and-interaction.md).\n\n## Custom renderers\n\nA `ChartRenderer` owns both deterministic server markup and one mounted\n`ChartSurface`. The surface renders scenes, converts browser coordinates to\nscene coordinates, paints focus, and releases renderer-owned resources.\n`mountChartRenderer` keeps responsive sizing, runtime updates, focus,\nkeyboard, tooltip, and selection behavior shared across renderers.\n\nCustom surfaces should resolve authored focus layers and data-less guides with\n`resolveFocusPresentation(scene, focus, pointer, cursor)`, then paint its\n`under` nodes, the base scene, and its `over` nodes in that order.\n\nThe scene compiler has already converted every mark-emitted `MarkFocusGuide`\nto a `SceneFocusGuide` with required `placement` before a renderer receives the\nscene. `resolveFocusPresentation` calls each guide's resolver with its local\nfocus, pointer, and cursor context. A custom renderer should consume those\nresolved nodes and final placement through that helper; it should not call\nguide resolvers or infer mark order again.\n\nUse `@tanstack/charts/renderer` directly or the framework `/core` entries.\nThe optional built-in implementation at `@tanstack/charts/canvas` demonstrates\nthe boundary without changing the default SVG imports.\n\n`ChartMarkRenderer` is the small renderer-selection token stored in universal\nmark and scene types. A DOM implementation uses `ChartLayerRenderer`, which\nextends `ChartRenderer` and `ChartMarkRenderer` with\n`compose(defaultRenderer)`. `UniversalChartLayerRenderer` provides the same\ncontract for a definition-agnostic renderer. The returned compositor owns the\nordered child surfaces and exposes them through `ChartSurface.layers`.\n\nBuilt-in marks accept a `ChartMarkRenderer` through their shared\n`ChartMarkOptions`. Custom marks pass it as the third factory argument, after\nthe optional motion definition. See\n[Mark-level renderers](./rendering-and-export.md#mark-level-renderers).\n\nFor an SVG-only serialization change, pass a `ChartSvgRenderer` as `renderSvg`\nto the compatibility host or adapt it with `createSvgChartRenderer` from\n`@tanstack/charts/svg/renderer`. Preserve the SVG root, stable DOM keys,\naccessible name, coordinate system, and focus presentation expected by that\nadapter.\n\nDefault SVG serialization already preserves declared gradients and group\nclips; see [Rendering and export](./rendering-and-export.md#svg-resources).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Custom Extensions\ndescription: Extend TanStack Charts with custom marks, distinct scale values, curves, scales, color, legends, text measurement, spatial indexes, and renderers.\n---\n\nTanStack Charts exposes narrow inversion-of-control boundaries around its\nscene compiler. Prefer composition with built-in marks first. Add an extension\nwhen the chart requires geometry or behavior that cannot be expressed without\ndistorting its data model.\n\n## Composite marks\n\n`compositeMark` groups ordinary marks behind one stable parent identity:\n\n```ts\nimport { compositeMark } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/composite'\n\nfunction compositeMark<\n  TMarks extends readonly ChartMark<any, any, any, any, any>[],\n>(\n  marks: TMarks,\n  options?: CompositeMarkOptions<ChartMarkDatum<TMarks[number]>>,\n): ChartMark<\n  ChartMarkDatum<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkPointX<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkPointY<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkScaleX<TMarks[number]>,\n  ChartMarkScaleY<TMarks[number]>\n>\n```\n\n`CompositeMarkOptions` contains optional `id`, `motion`, and `renderer` fields.\nThe result preserves the union of child datum and positional types. Initialization merges\nthe children's semantic channels under parent and child namespaces. Rendering\nkeeps child order, namespaces scene keys and mark IDs, and retains each child\npoint as a separate interaction target. Parent and child motion definitions\nmerge under the resolved child namespace, with child fields taking precedence.\n\nEvery child must have a unique ID and an ordinary initialized `render` method.\nA child that owns `resolveLayout` is rejected; keep one resolved-layout owner\ninstead of nesting scheduling lifecycles. See\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md#group-reusable-child-marks)\nfor composition guidance.\n\n## Custom marks\n\n```ts\nimport { createMark } from '@tanstack/charts'\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createMark<\n  TDatum,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TXScaleId extends string = 'x',\n  TYScaleId extends string = 'y',\n>(\n  initialize: (\n    context: MarkInitializeContext,\n  ) => MarkInitialization<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>,\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>,\n  renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer,\n): ChartMark<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue, TXValue, TYValue, TXScaleId, TYScaleId>\n```\n\nInitialization runs once per scene compilation and receives the mark's layer\nindex:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkInitializeContext {\n  markIndex: number\n}\n\ninterface InitializedMark<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  id: string\n  channels: Readonly<Record<string, MaterializedChannel>>\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<any>\n  viewport?: Readonly<Partial<Record<'x' | 'y', 'content' | 'fixed'>>>\n  focusGuideOnly?: boolean\n  layoutLabels?(context: MarkRenderContext): readonly SceneLabel[]\n  render(context: MarkRenderContext): MarkScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n  resolveLayout?(\n    context: MarkResolvedLayoutContext,\n  ): ResolvedMarkLayout<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n}\n```\n\n`MarkInitialization` also accepts a `ResolvedLayoutMarkInitialization`, which\nhas `resolveLayout` instead of an initial `render`. `createMark` normalizes both\nforms to `InitializedMark`, so wrappers around ordinary marks retain a callable\n`render`.\n\nThe optional factory `motion` is copied onto each initialized mark. An\ninitializer may return its own `motion` when a composite or resolved layout\nneeds a scene-local policy; that value takes precedence over the factory\nfallback.\n\nThe optional third `renderer` argument is copied onto the nodes returned by\nboth direct `render` and resolved-layout render paths. It does not replace or\nshift the motion argument. Use a stable `ChartMarkRenderer` instance so an\nupdate can reuse its existing surface composition.\n\nMaterialized channels declare semantic values before scale resolution:\n\n```ts\ninterface MaterializedChannel {\n  scale?: string\n  values: readonly unknown[]\n  includeZero?: boolean\n}\n```\n\nUse a `ChartSpec.scales` ID for a positional channel. The reserved `x` and `y`\nIDs are the defaults; additional IDs select named scales. Use `scale: 'color'`\nfor the shared color scale, and do not reuse `color` as a positional scale ID.\nPass named position IDs through the `createMark` scale ID type parameters so\ntheir values do not widen the reserved scale types. `includeZero` is a hint\navailable to a custom scale resolver. Filter invalid values before\nmaterializing them.\n\n`viewport` overrides presentation ownership independently for x and y. Without\nan override, a mark is viewport content on an axis when one of its materialized\nchannels uses that axis. `'content'` forces the mark into that axis's clipped,\ntranslated layer even when no channel establishes the relationship. `'fixed'`\nkeeps the mark stationary on that axis even when a channel contributes to its\ndomain. This ownership does not change scale-domain contribution.\n\nThe exported `ChartContinuousDomain<TValue>` type represents viewport state as\neither `readonly [number, number]` or `readonly [Date, Date]`, narrowed by the\nmark and axis value type. Numeric and temporal endpoints cannot be mixed.\n\nRender nodes and points with `context.scales[axis].map`. The scene compiler\napplies the mark's viewport translation and remaps its interaction-point\nreferences. `scale.viewport?.map` is for consumers that need the final\npresented coordinate outside mark rendering.\n\nThe render context provides final geometry and shared presentation:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkRenderContext {\n  markIndex: number\n  surface: ChartBounds\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  color(value: ChartKey | null | undefined): string\n  colors: ResolvedColorScale\n  layout: ChartLayoutOptions\n}\n```\n\n### Final-screen mark layout\n\nUse `resolveLayout` when binning, collision avoidance, topology, or responsive\npacking depends on final positional scales and inner bounds:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkResolvedLayoutContext {\n  markIndex: number\n  chart: ChartBounds\n  scales: Readonly<Record<string, ResolvedScale>>\n  theme: ChartTheme\n  layout: ChartLayoutOptions\n}\n\ninterface ResolvedMarkLayout<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue> {\n  channels?: Readonly<Record<string, MaterializedChannel>>\n  states?: InitializedMark<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>['states']\n  layoutLabels?(context: MarkRenderContext): readonly SceneLabel[]\n  render(context: MarkRenderContext): MarkScene<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>\n}\n```\n\nThe margin solver may call `resolveLayout` more than once. Keep it synchronous,\npure, deterministic, and free of application state. Initial channels alone\nestablish x/y domains. Resolved channels replace them for final\nnon-positional inference, including color; resolved x/y values never re-domain\nthe positional scales. Derived rows stay inside the returned render closure\ninstead of becoming a cross-mark transform graph.\n\nIf a custom mark emits labels that should participate in automatic margins,\nreturn the same positioned labels from `layoutLabels`. The solver may call it\nmore than once with different responsive ranges; keep it pure. `render` still\nruns once with the final layout. The built-in Cartesian `text` mark provides\nthis hook.\n\nReturn keyed scene nodes and, when the mark participates in native\ninteraction, typed points:\n\n```ts\ninterface MarkScene<\n  TDatum = unknown,\n  TXValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n  TYValue extends ChartValue = ChartValue,\n> {\n  nodes: readonly SceneNode[]\n  points?: readonly ChartPoint<TDatum, TXValue, TYValue>[]\n  focusAnchors?: readonly ChartFocusAnchor[]\n  focusGuides?: readonly MarkFocusGuide[]\n}\n\ntype MarkFocusGuide = Omit<SceneFocusGuide, 'placement'> & {\n  placement?: SceneFocusGuide['placement']\n}\n```\n\nFor a datum composed of several leaves, put state metadata on its `SceneGroup`\nand set `states.target: 'group'`. The resolver applies paint to that group once\nand leaves its children unchanged. This gives a candle's body and wicks one\nopacity transition instead of three. A child group with its own states still\nresolves independently. Omit `target`, or use `'children'`, for the existing\nstate inheritance behavior.\n\nGroup state metadata contains the original `data`, ordered `definitions`, and\nthe `points` that identify its datum. Those state candidates alone do not add\npointer or keyboard targets. Group states support paint; geometry properties\nsuch as dot radius and text rotation apply only to their corresponding leaves.\n\n`pointOwner` identifies one source point for decorative geometry. Use\n`pointOwners` for a fill or path owned by several points. These fields preserve\nsemantic ownership for state and focus resolution, they do not add hit-test\npoints. Scene adoption and composite namespacing transform them with the\ncorresponding source points.\n\nA `SceneLabel` may supply `focusOpacity(context)` for presentation-only\nopacity. The context contains current `focus` and `pointer`. Built-in tick\nlabels use this callback during focus painting, leaving their layout and\ncollision thinning unchanged. Their base style stores the initial opacity,\nwhich is restored when focus clears. Custom renderers must honor this scene\ncallback when implementing inline state painting.\n\nBrowser control extensions receive `setStateFocus(focus)` from their host.\nIt overrides only inline-state matching, not interaction focus, tooltip\ncontent, cursor state, or focus-filtered geometry. Pass `null` to restore\nmatching against real interaction focus. Controls own refreshing that override\nwhen their scene changes and clearing it on removal. The context and lifecycle\ntypes are exported as `ChartHostControlExtensionContext`,\n`ChartHostControlExtension`, and `ChartHostControlInstance`.\n\n`decorative(mark)` from `@tanstack/charts/mark/decorative` retains inline states\nand source ownership while removing pointer and keyboard targets. For a\ncomposite mark, wrap the complete composite rather than its children. A\ndecorative mark still cannot contain `whenFocused()` geometry, directly or\ninside a composite. These combinations are rejected by TypeScript when the\nmark's inferred type is retained. JavaScript consumers and custom marks with\nwidened types are checked at runtime during initialization or scene rendering.\n\n`focusAnchors` let `whenFocused` reveal decorative geometry without making it\na pointer or keyboard target. `focusGuides` describe data-less presentation\nresolved from current focus or cursor state. `MarkFocusGuide` has the final\n`SceneFocusGuide` fields except that `placement` is optional. Omit it to let\nmark order place a guide under or over the first ordinary mark. Supply it only\nwhen a composed nested scene must preserve an already resolved placement.\nEvery focus guide supplies a `resolve` callback that receives\n`SceneFocusGuideResolveContext` and returns one transient `SceneNode` or\n`undefined`. The callback stays attached to the optional guide instead of\nbecoming unconditional renderer policy. Import `resolveCrosshairGuide` from\n`@tanstack/charts/crosshair` to reuse the built-in rule, band, label, and marker\nbehavior.\n\nSet `focusGuideOnly: true` on an initialized mark that contributes only these\ndynamic guides and no ordinary base-scene content. This explicit\nclassification keeps the mark out of the first-ordinary-mark boundary used by\ndefault placement. `surface` is the required full-surface bounds; `chart` is\nthe inner plot. Guide rules normally clip to `chart`, while labels may use\n`surface` for clamping.\n\nThe practical contracts are in\n[Custom Marks and Renderers](../guides/custom-marks-and-renderers.md).\n\n### Mark requirements\n\n- Give the mark a stable ID. Derive a fallback from `markIndex` only when layer\n  order is stable.\n- Materialize every value needed to establish positional domains during\n  initialization.\n- Map through `context.scales`; do not recalculate responsive ranges.\n- Declare `viewport` ownership when channel inference does not match the\n  mark's content or fixed annotation behavior.\n- Give each scene node and point a deterministic key.\n- Keep presentation-only focus anchors keyed to the nodes they reveal.\n- Emit finite geometry only.\n- Preserve the original datum and index in every interaction point.\n- Use one honest focus coordinate and semantic x/y pair per point.\n- Keep semantic row transforms eager and outside `render`; use\n  `resolveLayout` only for work that requires final screen geometry.\n\nThe scene node and point shapes are documented in\n[Runtime and scene](./runtime-and-scene.md).\n\n## Distinct point and scale values\n\nInterval geometry may materialize endpoint value types that differ from its\ninteraction anchor types. Use the exceptional subpath:\n\n```ts\nimport { createMarkWithScaleValues } from '@tanstack/charts/mark/scale-values'\n```\n\n```ts\nfunction createMarkWithScaleValues<\n  TDatum,\n  TXPointValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYPointValue extends ChartValue,\n  TXScaleValue extends ChartValue,\n  TYScaleValue extends ChartValue,\n  TXScaleId extends string = 'x',\n  TYScaleId extends string = 'y',\n>(\n  initialize: (\n    context: MarkInitializeContext,\n  ) => MarkInitialization<TDatum, TXPointValue, TYPointValue>,\n  motion?: ChartMotionDefinition<TDatum>,\n  renderer?: ChartMarkRenderer,\n): ChartMark<\n  TDatum,\n  TXPointValue,\n  TYPointValue,\n  TXScaleValue,\n  TYScaleValue,\n  TXScaleId,\n  TYScaleId\n>\n```\n\nThe subpath also exports `ChartMarkPointX`, `ChartMarkPointY`,\n`ChartMarkScaleX`, and `ChartMarkScaleY`. Use it only when the distinction is\nreal; ordinary custom marks should use `createMark`. Pass named scale IDs in\nthe last two type parameters when the mark does not use reserved `x` or `y`.\n\n## Curves\n\n`ChartCurve` supplies precomputed path data for line and y-oriented area marks:\n\n```ts\ninterface ChartCurve {\n  line(points: readonly (readonly [number, number])[]): string\n  area(\n    top: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n    bottom: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n  ): string\n}\n```\n\n`AreaXCurve` has the transposed contract:\n\n```ts\ninterface AreaXCurve {\n  areaX(\n    right: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n    left: readonly (readonly [number, number])[],\n  ): string\n}\n```\n\nThe optional bridges `d3Curve` from `@tanstack/charts/d3/shape` and\n`d3AreaXCurve` from `@tanstack/charts/d3/area-x` adapt a supplied curve factory\nto these contracts. D3 module ownership and granular imports are documented in\n[Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Custom positional scales\n\nA custom `ChartScale` resolves semantic values and the responsive range into a\ncomplete mapping and tick set. This is an unchecked math boundary; prefer a\nconfigured callable scale when possible.\n\nSee [Custom scales](./scales-guides-and-color.md#custom-scales)\nfor the exact context and return type.\n\n## Custom color scales and legends\n\n`ChartColorScale` maps observed values, domain/range hints, and theme tokens to\na `ResolvedColorScale`. `ChartColorLegend` independently reserves layout height\nand emits a scene node.\n\nSee [Color](./scales-guides-and-color.md#color) and\n[Custom legends](./scales-guides-and-color.md#custom-legends).\n\n## Custom text measurement\n\n`ChartTextMeasurer` lets nonbrowser rendering, special fonts, or an\napplication-owned typography engine provide painted glyph bounds. It affects\nguide geometry, not mark text rendering. It is synchronous and receives the\ncomplete resolved `ChartTextTypography`; hosts re-render after asynchronous\nfont readiness changes.\n\nSee [Automatic guide layout](./scales-guides-and-color.md#automatic-guide-layout)\nfor the contract.\n\n## Spatial indexes\n\n`ChartSpatialIndexFactory` replaces the default linear pointer lookup without\nchanging scene compilation. Build a point-only index from its first argument,\nor use `context.scene` from its second argument to index resolved primitive\nbounds. Return the nearest original point within the requested distance. The\nhost recreates the index when the scene or factory changes.\n\nSee [Spatial indexes](./focus-and-interaction.md#spatial-indexes). The\nappropriate granular spatial primitive can be brought through the boundary\ndescribed in [Scales](../concepts/scales-and-d3.md).\n\n## Custom focus and gestures\n\n`ChartFocusStrategy` owns pointer resolution, focus grouping, and keyboard task\norder. Its `resolve(points, context)` and `group(points, context)` methods keep\ncoordinates and the active point in named context bags. Rich gestures can\ninstead disable chart-owned focus and maintain selection or viewport state in\nthe application.\n\nSee [Focus and interaction](./focus-and-interaction.md).\n\n## Custom renderers\n\nA `ChartRenderer` owns both deterministic server markup and one mounted\n`ChartSurface`. The surface renders scenes, converts browser coordinates to\nscene coordinates, paints focus, and releases renderer-owned resources.\n`mountChartRenderer` keeps responsive sizing, runtime updates, focus,\nkeyboard, tooltip, and selection behavior shared across renderers.\n\nCustom surfaces should resolve authored focus layers and data-less guides with\n`resolveFocusPresentation(scene, focus, pointer, cursor)`, then paint its\n`under` nodes, the base scene, and its `over` nodes in that order.\n\nThe scene compiler has already converted every mark-emitted `MarkFocusGuide`\nto a `SceneFocusGuide` with required `placement` before a renderer receives the\nscene. `resolveFocusPresentation` calls each guide's resolver with its local\nfocus, pointer, and cursor context. A custom renderer should consume those\nresolved nodes and final placement through that helper; it should not call\nguide resolvers or infer mark order again.\n\nUse `@tanstack/charts/renderer` directly or the framework `/core` entries.\nThe optional built-in implementation at `@tanstack/charts/canvas` demonstrates\nthe boundary without changing the default SVG imports.\n\n`ChartMarkRenderer` is the small renderer-selection token stored in universal\nmark and scene types. A DOM implementation uses `ChartLayerRenderer`, which\nextends `ChartRenderer` and `ChartMarkRenderer` with\n`compose(defaultRenderer)`. `UniversalChartLayerRenderer` provides the same\ncontract for a definition-agnostic renderer. The returned compositor owns the\nordered child surfaces and exposes them through `ChartSurface.layers`.\n\nBuilt-in marks accept a `ChartMarkRenderer` through their shared\n`ChartMarkOptions`. Custom marks pass it as the third factory argument, after\nthe optional motion definition. See\n[Mark-level renderers](./rendering-and-export.md#mark-level-renderers).\n\nFor an SVG-only serialization change, pass a `ChartSvgRenderer` as `renderSvg`\nto the compatibility host or adapt it with `createSvgChartRenderer` from\n`@tanstack/charts/svg/renderer`. Preserve the SVG root, stable DOM keys,\naccessible name, coordinate system, and focus presentation expected by that\nadapter.\n\nDefault SVG serialization already preserves declared gradients and group\nclips; see [Rendering and export](./rendering-and-export.md#svg-resources).\n"}
